An example of a long-term programmatic SEO project that won the May 2026 BCAU.

Many projects are affected by non-official Google update dates, not only official ones. If you have a programmatic SEO project that experienced a re-ranking around February 14–15, 2026, you may also see that it lost some rankings during the third week of March, then recovered and won the May 2026 BCAU.

You can use both official and non-official Google update dates to classify websites.

If a specific cluster of websites is affected by similar updates, either in the same direction or in the opposite direction, it usually means that these websites are being grouped together by the search engine.

In this example, the source had both Positive and Negative Ranking State signals, which created the “arch” pattern you see in the data.

For a long time, the website could not exceed a certain ranking threshold. But with the May 2026 BCAU, its Ranking State changed.

Ranking State means that the search engine places a website into a specific ranking scale. Until this state changes, the website usually cannot drop below or exceed certain ranking thresholds.

If your website exceeds a threshold but does not perform better in terms of clicks and user satisfaction, the next re-ranking can reverse that growth.

This is also the project I briefly explained before, where we moved all content into subdomains and improved the rankings after the website had been affected by the HCU.

One of the key changes was adding “buy” and “compare” functionality to the website, making it more useful and functional.

Technically, the HCU system is about whether your website has a real brand, purpose, and function.

A website should not rank only for the sake of ranking. It should rank because it serves a purpose and helps users complete something.

